  "explanation": "<h2>Present simple forms of <em>to be</em>: am / is / are</h2>\n<p>The verb <strong>to be</strong> is one of the most important verbs in English. At A1, you use it to talk about identity, feelings, places, and simple facts.</p>\n\n<h3>The forms</h3>\n<table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:1rem 0\">\n<thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;padding:0.4rem;border-bottom:1px solid #e5e7eb\">Subject</th><th style=\"text-align:left;padding:0.4rem;border-bottom:1px solid #e5e7eb\">Form</th><th style=\"text-align:left;padding:0.4rem;border-bottom:1px solid #e5e7eb\">Example</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td style=\"padding:0.4rem\">I</td><td style=\"padding:0.4rem\"><strong>am</strong></td><td style=\"padding:0.4rem\">I am a student.</td></tr>\n<tr><td style=\"padding:0.4rem\">He / She / It</td><td style=\"padding:0.4rem\"><strong>is</strong></td><td style=\"padding:0.4rem\">She is my sister.</td></tr>\n<tr><td style=\"padding:0.4rem\">You / We / They</td><td style=\"padding:0.4rem\"><strong>are</strong></td><td style=\"padding:0.4rem\">They are at home.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Short forms (contractions)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>I am → <strong>I'm</strong></li>\n<li>he is / she is / it is → <strong>he's / she's / it's</strong></li>\n<li>you are / we are / they are → <strong>you're / we're / they're</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In everyday American English, short forms are very common in speaking and informal writing.</p>\n\n<h3>Negatives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>I am not → <strong>I'm not</strong></li>\n<li>he/she/it is not → <strong>isn't</strong> (or <strong>is not</strong>)</li>\n<li>you/we/they are not → <strong>aren't</strong> (or <strong>are not</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Questions</h3>\n<p>Put <strong>am / is / are</strong> <em>before</em> the subject:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Are</strong> you okay?</li>\n<li><strong>Is</strong> she your teacher?</li>\n<li><strong>Am</strong> I late?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Short answers: Yes, I am. / No, she isn't. / Yes, they are.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>❌ <em>She are</em> my friend. → ✅ <strong>She is</strong> my friend.</li>\n<li>❌ <em>I is</em> tired. → ✅ <strong>I am</strong> tired.</li>\n<li>❌ <em>They is</em> here. → ✅ <strong>They are</strong> here.</li>\n<li>❌ <em>Is you</em> ready? → ✅ <strong>Are you</strong> ready?</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Quick tip</h3>\n<p>Think of the subject first. If you can replace it with <strong>he/she/it</strong>, use <strong>is</strong>. If you can replace it with <strong>they</strong>, use <strong>are</strong>. With <strong>I</strong>, always use <strong>am</strong>.</p>",
